# Advanced Splunk SPL Timechart & Transaction Auditing Report

## 1. Timechart Query Formulation & Error Spike Analysis

The Splunk `timechart` query is used to analyze request volume, successful requests, server-side errors, and average response duration in 15-minute intervals.

### A. SPL Timechart Query

index="ecommerce_checkout"
| timechart span=15m count AS Total_Requests count(eval(status=200)) AS Success_200 count(eval(status>=500 AND status<600)) AS Errors_5xx avg(duration_ms) AS Average_Duration_ms

### B. Timechart Summary Table

| Time Window (Span: 15m) | Total Requests | Success (200 OK) | Errors (5xx) | Average Duration (ms) | Severity Assessment |
|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|
| 10:00-10:15 | 4 | 4 | 0 | Normal | Informational |
| 10:15-10:30 | 5 | 4 | 1 | 1180.0 | Critical |
| 10:30-10:45 | 4 | 4 | 0 | 147.5 | Informational |
| 10:45-11:00 | 3 | 2 | 1 | 1713.3 | Critical |
| 11:00-11:15 | 4 | 4 | 0 | 187.5 | Informational |

### Analysis

The main error spikes occurred during the **10:15-10:30** and **10:45-11:00** windows. Each interval contains a 5xx error and a significantly higher average response duration.

The 10:15-10:30 interval has an average duration of approximately **1180 ms**, while the 10:45-11:00 interval reaches approximately **1713.3 ms**. This indicates that server-side failures are associated with increased transaction latency.

